<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policyconfig PUBLIC
  "-//freedesktop//DTD PolicyKit Policy Configuration 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/PolicyKit/1.0/policyconfig-dtd.txt">
<policyconfig>

  <!--
    RamSleuth — polkit policy for the one-click privileged setup helper.

    Purpose: make `pkexec ramsleuth-setup` (invoked by the in-app "Set up
    RamSleuth" wizard — plan C21-04/C21-06) prompt the active local user for
    a password and then run /usr/bin/ramsleuth-setup as root, in a single
    polkit action. That is what lets the GUI do its whole privileged
    one-shot (daemon enable+start, group join, socket ACL grant, and on AMD
    the offline DKMS build + modprobe) with one clean auth_admin prompt.
    This file is the in-repo source of truth / shared asset for the helper
    itself (scripts/ramsleuth-setup.sh, chunk C21-01).

    HOW pkexec RESOLVES THIS ACTION (verified against polkit 127 source —
    src/programs/pkexec.c find_action_for_path() — and the pkexec(8) man
    page, 2026-09-25; the version in Arch base at the time):
      * pkexec picks the first loaded action whose
        org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.path annotation equals the program
        path after realpath() resolution, and — only if that action also
        carries an org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.argv1 annotation — whose
        FIRST argument matches it. polkit has NO argument-list key: an
        'exec.arguments' annotation is not a thing (an empty one is inert
        dead weight, not a condition — do not add one back; it was removed
        2026-09-25 after it caused exactly that misdiagnosis).
      * This action therefore maps EVERY `pkexec /usr/bin/ramsleuth-setup`
        invocation — bare, with the user-name flag, with the DKMS flag
        (the C21-01 helper CLI: `user <name>`, `with-dkms`,
        `with-intel-dkms` in any order) — to the branded message below:
        auth_admin for any/active/inactive/other. exec.argv1 is
        deliberately absent: the helper's frozen CLI takes its flags in
        any order, so the first argument is not stable.
      * Caveat 1 (path): the match is against the realpath-resolved path,
        so /usr/bin/ramsleuth-setup must be a regular file (or this
        exec.path must be set to the symlink's target) — a symlinked
        helper would silently fall back to the generic
        org.freedesktop.policykit.exec dialog.
      * Caveat 2 (stale pool): a polkitd that started BEFORE this file was
        (re)installed may keep serving a stale action pool — polkit 127's
        inotify hot-reload did not pick up a freshly installed file in a
        live observation (2026-09-25: policy installed 3 min after boot; a
        pkexec 37 min later still authenticated the generic action, per
        polkitd's own journal line). The AUR packages' .install hooks
        (packaging/ramsleuth{,-bin}.install) and install.sh therefore run
        `systemctl restart polkit` after installing this file; a manual
        copy of the policy needs the same restart (or a reboot) before the
        branded dialog appears.

    Installation: NOT installed here. The packaging chunks copy this file to
    /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/90-ramsleuth-setup.policy:
      - C21-12  packaging/ramsleuth/PKGBUILD
      - C21-14  packaging/ramsleuth-bin/PKGBUILD (from the release tarball)
      - C21-16  install.sh (the self-contained manual installer)
    polkit is in the Arch `base` group, so the loader is present on every
    stock system (plan §6 risk 1).

    Results: auth_admin for any/active/inactive/other — admin (password)
    auth for the invoking local user, as the plan specifies.

    License: MIT (see the repo root LICENSE).
  -->

  <vendor>MadGoatHaz</vendor>
  <vendor_url type="href">https://github.com/MadGoatHaz/RamSleuth</vendor_url>
  <icon_name>ramsleuth</icon_name>

  <action id="org.freedesktop.ramsleuth.setup">
    <description>Run the RamSleuth one-click privileged setup</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to set up RamSleuth for this user. This enables and starts the RamSleuth daemon, grants your user access to its socket (so the app works immediately, without re-logging in), and — on AMD systems — builds and loads the ryzen_smu telemetry driver.</message>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>auth_admin</allow_any>
      <allow_active>auth_admin</allow_active>
      <allow_inactive>auth_admin</allow_inactive>
      <allow_other>auth_admin</allow_other>
    </defaults>
    <!-- Map pkexec to the installed helper: /usr/bin/ramsleuth-setup, any
         arguments. The match is exec.path-only (the realpath-resolved
         program path) — see the header for the full semantics. Leave
         exec.argv1 absent on purpose. -->
    <annotate>
      <key>org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.path</key>
      <value>/usr/bin/ramsleuth-setup</value>
    </annotate>
  </action>

</policyconfig>
